## What the analyzer is for

You are producing a DESIGNED mesh — authored, not scanned — and designed
meshes fail in a characteristic way. Scanned geometry has noise, holes
and chamfered features. Authored and procedurally generated geometry has
non-manifold edges, gaps between parts that were meant to meet, and
self-intersections. That is your defect list, and none of it is visible
in a render.

Degenerate near-zero-area triangles belong on the list for a different
reason: they break the normal, circumcenter and barycentric computations
that almost everything downstream depends on. Rare, and cheap to catch.

The gate measures STRUCTURE. It has never read the brief. It will pass a
sound mesh that is the wrong object.

## Repair is not a solution you can trust

Automatic repair is ill-posed and known to introduce new defects while
fixing the ones it was aimed at: a hole can be filled by many different
patches, a self-intersection resolved in more than one way, and the
algorithm has no way to know which was intended. Gap-closing is
threshold-bounded — it only guarantees a gap-free result for gaps
narrower than the threshold it was given, and a generous threshold
produces arbitrarily implausible geometry.

Consequences for how you work:

- Prefer building it correctly to building it and repairing it. Parts
  that must union should OVERLAP before the boolean; coplanar faces are
  the solver's worst case, and a gap you close afterwards is a guess.
- When you do run a cleanup, report the threshold you gave it. A cleanup
  whose assumption is not printed is a cleanup nobody can audit.
- Never describe a mesh as repaired. Describe what the analyzer measured
  after the repair.

## Reporting

Report the measurement, not the impression. "Manifold, one component,
812 triangles, no self-intersections" is a result. "Clean" is not.
